  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mary Vincent vs The State Of Telangana on 16 February, 2022",
    "facts": "This writ petition is filed by the wife of detenu-Vincent Kavin challenging the order of detention dated 26.09.2021 made under Section 3 (2) of the Telangana Prevention of Dangerous Activities of Bootleggers, Dacoits, Drug-Offenders, Goondas, Immoral Traffic Offenders, Land-Grabbers, Spurious Seed Offenders, Insecticide Offenders, Fertilizer Offenders, Food Adulteration Offenders, Fake Documents Offenders, Scheduled Commodities Offenders, Forest Offenders, Gaming Offenders, Sexual Offenders, Explosive Substances Offenders, Arms Offenders, Cyber Crime offenders and While Collar or Financial Offenders Act, 1986 (Act 1 of 1986). 3. Heard Sri Thomas Joseph Lloyd, learned counsel for the petitioner and learned Government Pleader for home. 4. The order of detention is passed by referring to three crimes registered against the detenu under Sections 380 and 457 of Indian Penal Code (IPC), read with Section 511 IPC in one case. According to learned counsel for the petitioner, last of the crimes was registered on 05.07.2021 and in all the three crimes, he was not even arrested by the police but notice under Section 41-A of Code of Criminal Procedure was issued. While so, and after more than two months the order of detention was made referring to the very same three instances. According to learned counsel, registration of three crimes does not warrant preventive detention.",
    "arguments": "It is appropriate to refer to the decision of the Hon'ble Apex Court in Rekha Vs State of Tamil Nadu1, wherein it is held as follows : \"Where a detention order is served on a person already in jail, there should be a real possibility of release of a person on bail who is already in custody, provided he has moved a bail application which is pending. It follows logically that if no bail application is pending, then there is no likelihood of the person in custody being released on bail, and hence, the detention order will be illegal.\" 12.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It is appropriate to refer to the decision of the Hon'ble Apex Court in Rekha Vs State of Tamil Nadu1, wherein it is held as follows : \"Where a detention order is served on a person already in jail, there should be a real possibility of release of a person on bail who is already in custody, provided he has moved a bail application which is pending. It follows logically that if no bail application is pending, then there is no likelihood of the person in custody being released on bail, and hence, the detention order will be illegal.\" 12."
  },
